  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT approving the exchange of certain lands between the Town of Long Lake and the People of the State of New York",
    "basePrintNo": "S4870",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4870-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4870\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act approving the exchange of certain lands between\nthe Town of Long Lake and the People of the State of New York\n\nPurpose of the Bill:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to implement the land exchange between the\nTown of Long Lake and the State of New York authorized by a 2007\nConstitutional Amendment.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 of this bill sets forth the legislative findings that the\nlegislature approves of the land exchange authorized by the bill and\nthat the provisions and requirements of the 2007 Constitutional\nAmendment, authorizing the Raquette Lake water supply land exchange,\nhave been satisfied.\n\nSection 2 of this bill directs the Commissioner of General Services,\nafter certification by the Commissioner of Environmental Conservation,\nto issue letters patent to the Town of Long Lake for the lands\ndescribed in section 5 of the bill and to convey such lands without\nreservation of any minerals.\n\nSection 3 of this bill requires that the title and deed of the land to\nbe conveyed to the State be approved by the Attorney General.\n\nSection 4 of this bill describes the approximate 12.2 acres of land to\nbe conveyed to the State from the Town of Long Lake.\n\nSection 5 of this bill describes the approximate one acre of land to\nbe conveyed to the Town of Long Lake from the State.\n\nSection 6 of this bill provides that the bill takes effect\nimmediately.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nSection 1 of Article XIV of the State Constitution preserves Forest\nPreserve as forever wild State lands and prohibits their lease, sale,\nexchange or taking. A Constitutional Amendment was ratified by the\nvoters at the general election in 2007 which authorized the State of\nNew York to convey one acre of land to the Town of Long Lake for\npurposes of establishing a drinking water well on the condition that\nin return the State receive at least twelve acres of land in Hamilton\nCounty for inclusion in the Forest Preserve.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nIn 2007, a Constitutional Amendment was adopted to allow the Town of\nLong Lake to receive one acre of land from the Forest Preserve for the\nsiting of a drinking water well. The well was necessary to comply with\n\n\nnew federal Safe Drinking Water Act regulations, which require public\nwater suppliers to provide adequate filtration and disinfection of\nwater that is taken from surface sources, or to develop an acceptable\nalternative source. As a result of those rules, the water supply\nsystem in the hamlet of Raquette Lake, which draws from a surface\nreservoir, could no longer be used to supply drinking water to that\nlocality.\n\nThe prevalence of State Forest Preserve lands surrounding this\ncommunity, coupled with the sanitary and hydrogeological requirements\nof public water supply wells, meant that the only viable alternative\nfor siting a drinking water supply well for the hamlet of Raquette\nLake was on Forest Preserve land. This legislation would authorize the\nlegal transfer of one acre of Forest Preserve land to the Town of Long\nLake for the purpose of siting a drinking water well to serve as the\nmunicipal drinking water supply for the hamlet of Raquette Lake.  This\nbill also authorizes the conveyance of 12.2 acres to the State for\nincorporation into the Forest Preserve. The lands to be conveyed to\nthe State are located adjacent to other Forest Preserve lands and have\nbeen appraised at a value greater than the one acre that will be\nconveyed to the Town.\n\nBudget implications:\n\nNone.\n\nLocal impact:\n\nThis bill requires the Town of Long Lake to provide at least 12 acres\nof land to the State; the land was donated by a Town Board member;\ntherefore this will have little fiscal impact on the Town and ensure\nit owns the land which is the location of its drinking water wells.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
